  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $\u03a0$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:{\\begin{aligned}x=3-5t \\\\ y=2-3t \\\\ z=-2-6t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,\u03a0:5x-2y+4z-132=0.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-2y+4z-132 & =0 \\\\ 5(3-5t)-2(2-3t)+4(-2-6t) & =132 \\\\ 15-25t-4+6t-8-24t & =132 \\\\ -43t & =129 \\\\ t & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=-3$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-5(-3)=18 \\\\ y=2-3(-3)=11 \\\\ z=-2-6(-3)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is  $P(18,11,16).$",
